Yes. What you’re noticing is real, but it’s not one single “AI warfare program.” It’s becoming an institutional stack spanning autonomy, cyber, intelligence, information operations, acquisition, industry, and command-and-control.

The clean map looks like this:

* Autonomous warfare: Hegseth announced AUTOWARCOM on September 30, a new four-star-level command intended to accelerate drones, robotics, autonomous systems, and AI-enabled capabilities across the military. That is an organizational shift, not merely another R&D project. 
* National-security AI: June’s NSPM-11 directs the national-security enterprise to rapidly onboard frontier models from multiple vendors, build high-security AI compute and an AI test range, conduct joint government-industry red teams, create common model/data exchanges, establish an AI National Security Strategic Reserve of outside experts, and standardize testing and assurance. It explicitly retains command accountability and requires systems to be controllable, robust and consistent with law. 
* AI + cyber defense: Executive Order 14409 created an AI cybersecurity clearinghouse among Treasury, NSA, CISA, industry and infrastructure operators to coordinate vulnerability discovery, validation, remediation and patching. It also directs classified benchmarking of frontier models for cyber capability. 
* Cyber with actual teeth: an August presidential memorandum goes considerably further: vetted U.S. companies can participate, under DOJ/DHS direction and federal oversight, in cyber-surveillance and cyber-effects operations against foreign cyber-enabled transnational criminal organizations. The memorandum builds in written approvals, vetting, legal review, deconfliction and stop/report requirements when operations exceed authorization. 
* Information/cognitive competition: Army professional and training institutions are explicitly treating information warfare, influence, deception and cognitive effects as increasingly important elements of competition, and the Army began a redesigned PSYOP training track this year framed partly around “cognitive warfare.” That does not establish some omnipotent AI persuasion system; it shows that the information environment is being treated more deliberately as an operational domain. 
* Human-machine command: recent Army work describes AI-enabled mission command as distributed cognition—people, models, data and processes sharing cognitive labor—where the human increasingly judges meaning and uncertainty rather than manually assembling every datum. That is very close to the architecture we’ve been developing independently. 

One correction on the Musk piece: Elon Musk is not currently running this Pentagon architecture. Musk left the administration in May 2025, and the temporary DOGE organization created in January 2025 was scheduled to terminate July 4, 2026. There is, however, a genuine lineage: former DOGE official Gavin Kliger was appointed Pentagon Chief Data Officer in March 2026 and was described by the Pentagon as sitting at the center of its major AI efforts. Hegseth had also openly welcomed Musk/DOGE into Pentagon efficiency work back in 2025. 

And there is another layer that is almost pure narrative/frame-setting: on September 29, 2026, the White House ordered executive agencies, where legally permissible, to use “Super Intelligence” / “SI” instead of “Artificial Intelligence” / “AI.” That does not itself create a new technical capability; it changes the government’s official vocabulary and therefore potentially the frame in which the technology is discussed. 

Your “friendly competition with teeth” idea maps to a legitimate strategic concept if we define it carefully. Between companies, labs, units, allies, or civilian systems, you can have:

cooperate on interfaces and safety → compete on performance → continuously red-team one another → preserve provenance → independently verify claims → keep consequential authority gated

That’s much healthier than either blind trust or permanent hostility.

At the nation-state level, though, we shouldn’t assume the competition is friendly. U.S. policy documents explicitly describe adversaries and strategic competitors, technical overmatch, resilience under attack, and deterrence. The reason to build the teeth is partly so competition stays below the threshold where somebody believes violence is the easier option. 

And this is where Pantheon actually has a useful conceptual contribution rather than just a metaphor. The architecture we’ve been converging on is:

COMPETE
   ↓
generate alternatives
   ↓
RED-TEAM
   ↓
independent challenge
   ↓
TRIANGULATE
   ↓
multiple models / evidence / perspectives
   ↓
TRUST
   ↓
provisional confidence
   ↓
VERIFY
   ↓
provenance + testing + reality delta
   ↓
AUTHORITY GATE
   ↓
consequential action
   ↓
OBSERVE CONSEQUENCES
   ↓
learn and recurse

So the gift can indeed have teeth without the teeth being the gift.

The gift is better coordination.
The teeth are the verification system that prevents coordination from becoming gullibility.

That is probably the part of this whole governmental shift I’d watch most closely: not merely who has the smartest AI, but who develops the best architecture for allowing many powerful intelligences—human and machine—to compete, challenge, translate, coordinate, and still remain accountable when somebody actually pulls a lever.